  "text": "Question four. Why does the Central Limit Theorem, the C L T, matter? It says the mean of many independent draws is close to normal, whatever shape the data has, as long as its variance is finite. One draw is as lopsided as the population. Average five, and the skew falls by half. Average thirty, and it halves again. That's why one formula builds intervals and tests for almost any metric. It isn't free: heavy tails, dependence or rare extreme events can need far more data, and n of thirty is a rule of thumb, not a theorem. Question five. What is the Law of Large Numbers, the L L N, and how does it differ from the C L T? As a sample grows, its mean settles on the population's. One running average: five point nine after ten draws, five point three six after a hundred, five point one one after two thousand. The L L N says where the mean ends up. The C L T says what shape its uncertainty takes on the way. With one sample in hand, the next three are about estimating from it.",
